            <ArticleTitle>Comparison of jaw-opening reflexes evoked by Er:YAG laser versus scalpel incisions in rats.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>31-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Anecdotal remarks suggest that incisions in oral soft tissue would be less painful if performed with laser rather than with scalpel, but such an argument remains to be scientifically validated.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="STUDY DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Twelve rats received graded incisions to lip, gingiva, mucosa, and tongue with either a pulsed Er:YAG laser (65 mJ/pulse at 10 Hz) or a stainless steel scalpel. The amplitude of the jaw-opening reflex, as measured by the digastric muscle electromyogram, was used to quantify the nociceptive response evoked by the surgical incisions.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Except for lip, mean reflex amplitudes evoked by laser were significantly smaller (P &lt; .05) than those evoked by scalpel in all other incision sites.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Er:YAG laser surgery may be less painful and therefore require less use of anesthesia and sedation than conventional scalpel surgery in oral soft tissue procedures.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
